{
  id:'npqb-nr-251', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'Management of choice in Guillain-Barre syndrome is:',
  options:['Immunoglobulin', 'Cyclophosphamide', 'Corticosteroid', 'Interferon'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Immunoglobulin is the keyed treatment. The peripheral neuropathy lecture states that early plasmapheresis or IVIG accelerates recovery and diminishes the incidence of long-term neurologic disability in Guillain-Barre syndrome, and that early plasmapheresis or IVIG therapy has proved useful in GBS. Both intravenous immunoglobulin and plasma exchange are therefore the evidence-backed acute treatments in the course material, so immunoglobulin is the intended management of choice among the four listed options.\n\nCorticosteroid is not selected even though it is often used in autoimmune neurology, because the lecture\'s GBS treatment section names plasmapheresis and IVIG, not steroids, as the useful early therapies. The claim that corticosteroids are ineffective or potentially harmful in GBS is not taken from the course material. Cyclophosphamide is an immunosuppressant used in some immune-mediated conditions, but the course does not mention it in GBS management, so any specific role or effectiveness is not taken from the course material. Interferon is likewise absent from the lecture\'s GBS treatment section; it is a cytokine with immunomodulatory actions, but its use in GBS is not taken from the course material.\n\nThe lecture supports the diagnostic framework around the answer too: GBS is immune mediated, with inflammatory pathology, and patients improve with immunomodulatory therapy such as plasmapheresis or IVIG. The treatment works through the disease\'s immune mechanism rather than by any direct antimicrobial or antiviral action. Among the four options only immunoglobulin belongs to the lecture\'s named useful therapies, which is why it is the management of choice. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify IVIG as a first-line immunomodulatory treatment for Guillain-Barre syndrome.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q251, book p.50'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-252', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'A 35- year- old clerk presents with diplopia and fatigue. Her symptoms are worse towards the evening. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Myasthenia gravis', 'Peripheral neuritis', 'Motor neurone disease', 'Guillain-Barre syndrome'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Myasthenia gravis is the keyed diagnosis. The muscle disease lecture describes MG as an autoimmune disease with a disorder of neuromuscular transmission (post-synaptic), manifested by fluctuating muscle fatigability and weakness that improve with rest, and it states that peaks of onset are seen in the 2nd and 3rd decades in females and in the 5th and 6th decades in males — the 35-year-old clerk\'s age falls inside the lecture\'s stated female peak. The lecture lists intermittent and asymmetric extra-ocular muscle affection with diplopia and ptosis among the cardinal symptoms, and states that fatigability is worse at the end of the day and with exercise with transient improvement with rest, which is exactly the diplopia and evening-worsening fatigue described in the stem. Mechanistically, the lecture explains MG as an autoimmune disease mediated by T-lymphocytes with autoantibodies to nicotinic acetylcholine receptors of the post-synaptic neuromuscular junction, or against muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK), which block acetylcholine binding to its receptor or trigger immune-mediated receptor degradation; the resulting loss of functional ACh receptors produces the fatigable transmission failure that worsens across a day of sustained use. The lecture\'s diagnostic approach names the edrophonium (Tensilon) test, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that transiently improves weakness, as the confirmatory next step for a presentation like this one, though it is not performed in the stem.\n\nGuillain-Barre syndrome is wrong because the peripheral neuropathy lecture defines it as an acute onset of peripheral and cranial nerve dysfunction, usually preceded by a viral respiratory or gastrointestinal infection 5 days to 4 weeks earlier, with rapidly progressive symmetric weakness, loss of tendon reflexes, and facial diplegia. This patient has no antecedent illness, no areflexia, and no symmetric limb weakness; instead she has fluctuating ocular-fatigability symptoms with evening worsening, which does not fit the GBS picture or its monophasic days-to-weeks progressive course taught in the lecture. Peripheral neuritis is not a lecture diagnosis; the course material discusses peripheral neuropathy and polyneuropathy as diffuse peripheral nerve disorders with weakness, sensory loss, pain, and autonomic dysfunction that are typically distal and symmetric, none of which fits isolated diplopia and fatigue without limb or sensory involvement. Motor neurone disease is not selected because the muscle disease lecture places progressive spinal muscular atrophy (MND) in the differential of muscle dystrophy as a progressive, non-fluctuating anterior horn cell disorder, and the same lecture\'s MG differential separately lists ALS as an alternative precisely because it lacks fluctuating, rest-responsive fatigability; MND does not improve with rest the way this patient\'s evening-worsening symptoms imply a rest-responsive process would. The fluctuating diurnal pattern with ocular onset, keyed to the lecture\'s MG description and its typical age and sex distribution, is the decisive feature. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize fluctuating ocular weakness worse at day-end as myasthenia gravis.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q252, book p.50'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-253', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'A 40-year-old man presents with a recent history of progressive weakness in the arms and legs after an episode of diarrhoea. Examination revealed flaccid weakness of limbs with no reflexes. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Guillain Barre syndrome', 'Motor neuron disease', 'multiple sclerosis', 'myasthenia gravis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Guillain Barre syndrome is the keyed diagnosis. The peripheral neuropathy lecture states that the Guillain-Barre syndrome is characterized by acute onset of peripheral and cranial nerve dysfunction, with a viral respiratory or gastrointestinal infection often preceding neurologic symptoms by 5 days to 4 weeks. The patient had diarrhoea before developing progressive arm and leg weakness, an antecedent and timing that match. The lecture also describes rapidly progressive symmetric weakness and loss of tendon reflexes as part of the GBS syndrome, and the stem\'s flaccid weakness with absent reflexes fits the taught pattern of a flaccid areflexic patient in GBS. The increased age with progression after a GI infection is the classic story the course depicts.\n\nMotor neuron disease is wrong because the lecture describes MND as an anterior horn cell progressive muscular atrophy, a chronic progressive disorder rather than an acute post-infectious presentation, and it does not follow a diarrhoeal episode. Multiple sclerosis is not selected; the lecture discusses MS only in the differential diagnosis of myasthenia gravis, and a post-infectious flaccid areflexic limb weakness is not the MS presentation described there. Myasthenia gravis is wrong because MG is fluctuating fatigability with improvement on rest, ocular and bulbar involvement, and it is not provoked by preceding diarrhoea. The key contrast is the acute arreflexic flaccid weakness after a gastrointestinal infection, which points to GBS according to the lecture\'s definition. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize post-infectious flaccid areflexic weakness as Guillain-Barre syndrome.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q253, book p.50'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-254', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'A 54-year-old female is admitted with progressive weakness following a trivial flu-like illness. Which of the following would EXCLUDE Guillain-Barre Syndrome as the diagnosis?',
  options:['Autonomic dysfunction', 'Elevated protein on CSF examination', 'Evidence of muscle wasting', 'Ophthalmoplegia', 'Sensory level below T 1'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'The printed box says: Explanation: sensory level is NOT a feature and would suggest cervical myelopathy. Muscle wasting is typical with prolonged illness. The keyed option E, Sensory level below T 1, excludes GBS because a discrete sensory level is a sign of a spinal cord lesion such as cervical myelopathy, not of the diffuse peripheral nerve process that GBS represents. A sensory level implies a horizontal band on the trunk below which all sensory modalities are lost, which localizes to a transecting cord lesion; cervical myelopathy would also be expected to show upper motor neuron signs below the lesion such as hyperreflexia and extensor plantars, the opposite of the hyporeflexic, diffusely areflexic pattern GBS produces, though that specific upper-motor-neuron contrast is not taken from the course material. The box states the sensory-level exclusion expressly, and it is quoted verbatim above.\n\nThe lecture supports the surrounding framework. GBS is an acute onset peripheral and cranial nerve dysfunction with symmetric limb weakness, loss of tendon reflexes, facial diplegia, oropharyngeal and respiratory paresis, and impaired sensation in hands and feet in a stocking-and-glove distribution. A stocking-and-glove sensory pattern is distal and symmetric, arising from the diffuse peripheral nerve process the lecture describes histologically as focal segmental demyelination with perivascular and endoneurial lymphocytic infiltrates scattered through nerves, nerve roots, and cranial nerves; this is a length-dependent, generalized process, not a lesion confined to one spinal level. A sensory level at T1, by contrast, is a localized trunk landmark that implies a lesion transecting the cord at a single site. The lecture\'s GBS sensory description therefore does not include, and structurally cannot produce, a sensory level.\n\nAutonomic dysfunction does not exclude GBS because the lecture states that autonomic dysfunction, including orthostatic hypotension, labile blood pressure, and tachyarrhythmia, is frequent in more severe GBS cases and is an important cause of morbidity and mortality; it is an expected accompaniment, not a contradiction. Elevated protein on CSF examination does not exclude GBS because the lecture\'s diagnostic definition of GBS includes an elevated CSF protein content with normal cell count as part of what defines the syndrome, so a raised protein is confirmatory rather than exclusionary. Evidence of muscle wasting is not the excluded feature either; the box explains that muscle wasting is typical with prolonged illness, meaning it reflects disuse atrophy accumulating over a prolonged flaccid paralysis rather than a sign pointing away from GBS. Ophthalmoplegia does not exclude GBS because the lecture notes that more than 50% of patients have facial diplegia and that cranial nerves, including the ocular muscles, are particularly affected in GBS; ocular involvement is therefore compatible with, not against, the disease. The single finding that genuinely contradicts the GBS pattern is the discrete sensory level, which the box assigns instead to cervical myelopathy.',
  objective:'Recognize that a discrete sensory level excludes GBS and suggests myelopathy.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q254, book p.50'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-255', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'36 year-old lady presents with a 3 day history of slurred speech. She also complains of double vision and a sensation of breathlessness. The Oxygen saturations are 99% on air. Neurological examination reveals normal power, reflexes and plantar responses. Which of the following would improve symptoms in this presentation?',
  options:['Edrophonium', 'Immunoglobulin', 'Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs', 'Physiotherapy', 'Plasmapheresis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'The printed box says: Explanation: This lady has symptoms suggesting MG. Her breathlessness is probably secondary to anxiety about the symptoms and associated hyperventilation. Tensilon is sometimes used in the diagnosis of MG and would be expected to temporarily improve symptoms. The keyed option Edrophonium is therefore the answer, because edrophonium (Tensilon) temporarily improves myasthenic weakness, and the box states that Tensilon is used in MG diagnosis and would be expected to temporarily improve symptoms. The muscle disease lecture gives the dose and mechanism behind this: edrophonium is an acetylcholine esterase inhibitor given intravenously as 10 mg/1 ml, starting with a 2 mg test dose then 8 mg over one minute, and because MG weakness arises from loss of functional post-synaptic acetylcholine receptors, transiently blocking acetylcholinesterase raises acetylcholine concentration at the junction and produces a brief, objective improvement in strength — the mechanism the box is describing. The lecture also lists acetylcholine receptor antibodies as positive in 90% of patients, with striated muscle antibodies positive in 50% of patients with ocular myasthenia, as further diagnostic tools that would corroborate a positive edrophonium response in a presentation like this one.\n\nThe muscle disease lecture\'s cardinal symptoms match the vignette point for point: intermittent and asymmetric extra-ocular muscle affection with diplopia and ptosis, and soft speech with a nasal tone, nasal regurgitation, and difficult chewing due to bulbar affection. The patient\'s slurred speech and double vision reproduce this bulbar-plus-ocular pattern, and the box confirms the presentation suggests MG. Because the lady\'s oxygen saturations are 99% on air and her reflexes and power are normal, respiratory muscle weakness is not the acute problem; the lecture lists respiratory muscle involvement with respiratory failure as a cardinal MG symptom in severe disease, but this patient shows none of it. The breathlessness is instead interpreted in the box as anxiety-related hyperventilation, so emergency immunomodulation is not the intended action here.\n\nImmunoglobulin and Plasmapheresis are effective acute GBS and MG-crisis treatments in the lectures — the muscle disease lecture lists plasma exchange as indicated in myasthenic crisis, steroid-related exacerbation, maintenance therapy in refractory patients, and before thymectomy, and IVIG at 0.4 mg/kg/day for 5 days — but the box frames this presentation as MG symptoms with anxiety-driven breathlessness rather than a crisis, and the specific question is which agent improves symptoms in this presentation rather than which agent treats a crisis. Edrophonium is the diagnostically active agent that transiently improves the weakness right now. Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and Physiotherapy are not MG treatments in either lecture — NSAIDs have no taught role in a neuromuscular junction disorder, and physiotherapy is described as a supportive measure for peripheral nerve disease in the peripheral neuropathy lecture, not a symptom-improving MG intervention — so their role here is not taken from the course material. The key asks what improves symptoms now, and only edrophonium matches the box\'s diagnostic-improvement claim.',
  objective:'Recognize that edrophonium transiently improves myasthenic symptoms.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q255, book p.50'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-256', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'Porphyria can cause:',
  options:['Motor polyneuropathy.', 'Acute polymyositis', 'Diffuse myelopathy', 'Paraplegia with sensory level'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Motor polyneuropathy is the keyed answer. The peripheral neuropathy lecture\'s immune-mediated table lists Guillain-Barre and variants with urine porphyrins among the laboratory tests, and the hereditary table lists porphyria under "Other" hereditary causes with urine porphyrins as the laboratory marker. Porphyria is therefore presented in the course material as a cause of peripheral neuropathy, and its porphyrin-based testing places it in the neuropathy family. Motor polyneuropathy, meaning a neuropathy predominantly affecting motor fibres, is the lecture-supported association among the four options; the claim that porphyria produces a specifically motor-predominant neuropathy rather than a mixed one is not taken from the course material and is read as the bank\'s intended association.\n\nAcute polymyositis is wrong because the muscle disease lecture classifies inflammatory/immune-mediated myositis and dermatomyositis as acquired myopathies; porphyria is not listed as a cause of myositis in either lecture, so any link between porphyria and polymyositis is not taken from the course material. Diffuse myelopathy is wrong because a myelopathy is a spinal cord disorder, and neither lecture places porphyria among myelopathies; the neuropathy table groups porphyria with peripheral nerve, not cord, disease. Paraplegia with sensory level is likewise a cord-type syndrome that the lectures do not attribute to porphyria; a sensory level would instead suggest a cord lesion, consistent with the boxed teaching in this topic that a sensory level suggests cervical myelopathy. The course\'s placement of porphyria in the neuropathy tables selects motor polyneuropathy as the answer. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate porphyria, as clustered in the lecture tables, with motor polyneuropathy.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q256, book p.50'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-257', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'A 50-year-old woman is referred with a two-week history of walking difficulty and weakness in her arms. On examination, there was proximal and distal limb weakness which is more marked, in the legs than the arms. All tendon reflexes were absent and the plantar responses were flexor. There was no sensory loss. Blood pressure in the supine position was 140/75 mmHg (lying) and was 1 10/70 mmHg on standing. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cervical cord compression', 'Guillain-Barre syndrome', 'Myasthenia gravis', 'Poliomyelitis', 'Polymyositis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Guillain-Barre syndrome is the keyed diagnosis. The peripheral neuropathy lecture identifies autonomic dysfunction, including orthostatic hypotension, labile blood pressure, and tachyarrhythmia, as frequent in more severe GBS cases and an important cause of morbidity and mortality. The patient\'s blood pressure falls from 140/75 mmHg supine to 110/70 mmHg on standing, an orthostatic drop, which points to the autonomic involvement the lecture places in GBS. Her absent tendon reflexes match the lecture\'s statement that tendon reflexes are lost in GBS, and her proximal and distal limb weakness with legs more affected than arms fits the rapidly progressive symmetric weakness and loss of tendon reflexes that define the GBS syndrome; the lecture also notes that, in contrast to most other neuropathies, proximal muscles are sometimes initially affected more than distal muscles in GBS, which matches this combined proximal-and-distal pattern rather than a purely distal, length-dependent neuropathy. The two-week course of worsening is also compatible with the taught course of GBS worsening for several days to three weeks, followed by a period of stability and then gradual improvement. Mechanistically, the lecture describes GBS as immune-mediated, with inflammatory pathology and improvement with immunomodulatory therapy, arising through molecular mimicry between cross-reactive epitopes of Campylobacter jejuni and peripheral nerve; the diagnosis is confirmed by compatible electrophysiology and an elevated CSF protein content with a normal cell count, which this vignette does not report but which would be the expected next test.\n\nCervical cord compression is wrong because the plantar responses are recorded as flexor; an upper motor neuron lesion from cord compression would be expected to produce extensor plantars, spasticity, and often a sensory level, which the stem explicitly denies by reporting flexor plantars and no sensory loss. Myasthenia gravis is wrong because MG presents with fluctuating fatigability that improves with rest and is not typically arreflexic, whereas this patient has fixed, non-fluctuating absent tendon reflexes and progressive symmetric weakness without diurnal variation, the opposite of the lecture\'s MG pattern. Poliomyelitis is wrong because the lecture lists acute poliomyelitis only as a differential of GBS without detailing its own presentation; the specific poliomyelitis clinical picture is not taken from the course material, but the pattern of symmetric ascending weakness with orthostatic autonomic dysfunction fits the lecture\'s GBS description far better than an isolated lower motor neuron polio picture, which classically spares sensation but would not typically produce the autonomic blood-pressure swing seen here. Polymyositis is an inflammatory myopathy with muscle weakness in the muscle disease lecture\'s acquired myopathy list, but that lecture does not give polymyositis arreflexia or orthostatic hypotension; polymyositis weakness is a primary muscle-fibre process rather than a peripheral-nerve process, so it would not be expected to abolish tendon reflexes or cause autonomic instability, which are the decisive GBS features here. The combination of lost reflexes, symmetric ascending proximal-and-distal weakness, and orthostatic hypotension selects GBS over all four distractors. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize GBS from areflexia and orthostatic hypotension after acute weakness.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q257, book p.51'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-258', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'36-year-old man has a 3-Ms history of pain in his feet & lower legs, was diagnosed as having diabetes at age 14 and treated with insulin. He is a cannabis smoker and drinks 30 units of alcohol per week. On examination he has impaired pain and temperature sensation in feet and lower legs, normal joint position and vibration sense. His reflexes are normal. What is diagnosis?',
  options:['Alcoholic polyneuropathy.', 'Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP).', 'Diabetic polyneuropathy.', 'Syringomyelia.', 'Vitamin B 12 deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'The printed box reads, verbatim: Explanation: The history suggests small fibres peripheral sensory neuropathy. The commonest cause is DM. Vitamin B 12 deficiency causes impairment of joint position and vibration. (CIDP) causes a large fibre peripheral neuropathy with areflexia, with alcoholic polyneuropathy all fibres types are affected and — the bank\'s own printed explanation ends mid-sentence after "affected and"; it is preserved here exactly as printed and is not completed or guessed.\n\nDiabetic polyneuropathy is the keyed answer because the box names diabetes mellitus as the commonest cause of a small-fibre peripheral sensory neuropathy, and the patient is an insulin-treated diabetic diagnosed at age 14, meaning roughly two decades of disease duration by the time of this presentation. The peripheral neuropathy lecture states that peripheral neuropathy occurs in approximately 50% of patients with diabetes mellitus, most commonly as a distal symmetric neuropathy, and that painful neuropathies, mononeuropathies, and radiculopathies with a disabling and continuous pain, often a burning sensation in a stocking distribution, occur in diabetes; it also attributes the progressive symmetric polyneuropathy to microvascular disease resulting from hyperglycaemia. The patient\'s impaired pain and temperature sensation in feet and lower legs with preserved joint position and vibration is the small-fibre pattern the box describes, and the pain in feet and lower legs matches the lecture\'s painful distal diabetic neuropathy. The distinction between small unmyelinated fibres, which carry pain and temperature, and large myelinated fibres, which carry vibration and joint-position sense, is standard peripheral-nerve physiology and is not taken from the course material, but it is the physiological basis for why this patient\'s dissociated sensory pattern is called small-fibre.\n\nAlcoholic polyneuropathy is a strong distractor because the patient drinks 30 units of alcohol weekly, but the box says alcoholic polyneuropathy affects all fibre types, whereas this patient shows selective small-fibre involvement with normal vibration and joint position; a genuinely alcoholic neuropathy would be expected to impair vibration and proprioception too, which it does not here. Neither lecture separately details alcoholic peripheral neuropathy beyond the box\'s own statement, so its all-fibre-type claim is taken directly from the box rather than from the lecture material. CIDP is wrong because the box says it causes a large-fibre peripheral neuropathy with areflexia, and this patient has normal reflexes and a small-fibre pattern rather than the large-fibre, areflexic picture CIDP produces. Syringomyelia produces a dissociated suspended sensory loss in a cape distribution over the trunk and arms from damage to crossing spinothalamic fibres around the central canal; the box does not use it, and its specific presentation is not taken from the course material, but it would not in any case match a distal feet-and-lower-legs distribution. Vitamin B 12 deficiency causes impaired joint position and vibration, which are normal in this patient, as the box states; B12 deficiency classically affects the large, myelinated dorsal-column fibres rather than the small pain-and-temperature fibres this patient has lost. The cannabis use in the stem is not linked to neuropathy in either lecture or in the box, so it carries no diagnostic weight here. The selective small-fibre loss in a longstanding insulin-treated diabetic is the decisive finding.',
  objective:'Recognize small-fibre-predominant sensory loss in diabetes as diabetic polyneuropathy.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q258, book p.51'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-259', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'A 25-year-old lady recently diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis developed weakness, double vision and tiredness. Examination reveals bilateral weakness of eye abduction, bilateral ptosis, slightly reduced proximal motor power in the limbs, normal reflexes and sensation. What is your diagnosis?',
  options:['Chronic external ophthalmoplegia.', 'Guillain-Barre syndrome.', 'Multiple sclerosis.', 'Myasthenia gravis.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'The printed box says: Explanation: MG is well known to be associated with other autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis. In Guillain-Barre syndrome you will expect absent reflexes. Myasthenia gravis is the keyed diagnosis, and the box gives the two decisive reasons: MG associates with other autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis, and GBS would be expected to show absent reflexes, which this patient does not have.\n\nThe muscle disease lecture supports both points. It lists diplopia and ptosis among the cardinal symptoms of MG, with intermittent and asymmetric extra-ocular muscle affection, fluctuating weakness of extra-ocular, bulbar, and extremity muscles, and proximal limb weakness; this patient has bilateral ptosis, bilateral weakness of eye abduction (extra-ocular weakness), and slightly reduced proximal motor power, reproducing that pattern closely. The lecture states MG has a prevalence of about 1 in 100,000, is an autoimmune disease mediated by T-lymphocytes with autoantibodies to nicotinic acetylcholine receptors of the post-synaptic neuromuscular junction (or against MuSK), and that it may be associated with other autoimmune diseases in about 10% of cases, naming hyperthyroidism, Hashimoto thyroiditis, pernicious anaemia, SLE, and rheumatoid arthritis specifically. The recent rheumatoid-arthritis diagnosis therefore fits the MG-disease association exactly, by name, in the lecture\'s own list. The lecture\'s Osserman classification places predominantly ocular disease in group I (15-20% of cases) and mild generalized disease with limb involvement in group IIa (30% of cases); this patient\'s combination of bilateral ocular weakness with mild proximal limb weakness places her presentation in the mild generalized category rather than pure ocular MG.\n\nGuillain-Barre syndrome is wrong because the peripheral neuropathy lecture describes GBS as having loss of tendon reflexes as a defining feature, and this patient\'s reflexes are normal; the box makes the same point as printed above. Multiple sclerosis appears in the muscle disease lecture only as the second item in the differential-diagnosis list of MG, without its own presenting features being taught, so the course does not provide an MS pattern that fits bilateral ptosis with preserved reflexes and a recent autoimmune diagnosis; MS is excluded by absence of supporting course content rather than by a contrasting feature. Chronic external ophthalmoplegia is not named as such in either lecture, but the muscle disease lecture does describe a related entity, oculo-pharyngeal muscular dystrophy, as autosomal dominant with onset in mid-to-late adulthood (3rd to 4th decade) presenting with ptosis, dysphagia, ophthalmoplegia, and mild proximal weakness — surface features overlapping this option. That lecture entity is a slowly progressive inherited myopathy, however, with no taught association to rheumatoid arthritis or to an acute-onset autoimmune trigger, so it does not explain this patient\'s recent-onset presentation alongside newly diagnosed rheumatoid arthritis; the exact identity between the answer option and this lecture entity is not confirmed by the course material. The normal reflexes plus the explicit rheumatoid-arthritis association select MG over all three distractors.',
  objective:'Recognize MG from ocular weakness with normal reflexes in an autoimmune patient.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q259, book p.51'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-260', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'A 1 6-year-old boy presents with rapidly progressive weakness over three days, which is ascending from his lower limbs upwards, associated with areflexia. but no sensory loss or bladder disturbances. Which one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?',
  options:['Azathioprine', 'Cyclosporin', 'Immunoglobulin', 'Methylprednisolone'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'Immunoglobulin is the keyed treatment. The boy\'s rapidly progressive ascending weakness with areflexia over three days is the GBS pattern the peripheral neuropathy lecture describes: rapidly progressive symmetric weakness, loss of tendon reflexes, and orthostatic or autonomic features in severe cases, with the condition worsening for several days. The lecture\'s treatment section states that early plasmapheresis or IVIG accelerates recovery and diminishes the incidence of long-term neurologic disability, and that early plasmapheresis or IVIG therapy has proved useful in GBS. Intravenous immunoglobulin is therefore one of the two named first-line acute therapies, and it is the intended option here.\n\nAzathioprine is wrong for acute GBS because the muscle disease lecture gives azathioprine under myasthenia gravis management at 100-150 mg/day, not for an acute ascending polyneuropathy; its role in GBS is not taken from the course material. Cyclosporin is likewise listed only under MG management in the muscle disease lecture, so any use in GBS is not taken from the course material. Methylprednisolone is not selected because the lecture\'s GBS treatment does not name corticosteroids; its effectiveness or harm in GBS is not taken from the course material. The key contrast is acute immunomodulation with IVIG (or plasma exchange) for GBS versus long-term immunosuppressants used in MG. The lecture\'s explicit naming of IVIG as useful early GBS therapy makes it the most appropriate of the four. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Choose immunoglobulin as an early first-line therapy for acute GBS.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q260, book p.51'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-261', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'Which of the followings is a common cause of coarse fasciculation?',
  options:['Muscle Dystrophy', 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Cortical infarction', 'Motor neuron disease'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'Motor neuron disease is the keyed answer. The peripheral neuropathy lecture defines fasciculations as spontaneous contractions of individual motor units, visible twitches of limb or cranial muscles. The muscle disease lecture lists progressive spinal muscular atrophy and motor neuron disease (MND) together as the clinical differential of the dystrophies, placing MND among anterior horn cell disorders, and it is that anterior horn cell pathology that gives the coarse fasciculations the bank intends; the claim that MND produces specifically coarse rather than fine fasciculation is not taken from the course material and is read as the bank\'s intended association. Fasciculation is therefore a motor-unit phenomenon, and the lectures link it to motor neuron involvement, while the other listed options are not the coarsely fasciculating disease.\n\nMuscle Dystrophy is wrong because the lecture on Duchenne dystrophy says expressly that there is no fasciculation in that condition, so primary muscle disease is distinguished from anterior horn cell disease by this finding. Peripheral neuropathy is wrong because the lecture describes polyneuropathy largely through weakness, sensory loss, and reflex loss; the claim that peripheral neuropathy commonly produces coarse fasciculation is not taken from the course material. Cortical infarction is an upper motor neuron lesion; the lectures do not associate cortical infarction with fasciculation, since fasciculation is a lower motor neuron motor-unit sign, so its role here is not taken from the course material. The anterior horn cell localization of MND, with the lecture defining fasciculation as motor-unit twitching, selects motor neuron disease. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate coarse fasciculation with motor neuron disease as an anterior horn cell disorder.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q261, book p.51; also printed as Q280 (book p.53), options reshuffled, key printed there as \'B\' — same content, folded, not drafted separately'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-262', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'Which of the followings is associated with marked delay in nerve conduction velocity:',
  options:['Muscle disease', 'Demyelinating polyneuropathy', 'Neuromuscular junction', 'Anterior horn cell disease'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Demyelinating polyneuropathy is the keyed answer. The peripheral neuropathy lecture states that nerve conduction velocities are reduced in GBS, and it links demyelination to slowed conduction by describing GBS as a demyelinating neuropathy with focal segmental demyelination on histology. The broader claim that a marked delay in nerve conduction velocity is the electrophysiological signature of demyelinating polyneuropathy is not taken from the course material but is the intended association, and the course\'s GBS statement that conduction velocities are reduced in a demyelinating disease anchors it. The lecture likewise uses "demyelinating" as a neuropathy category, and reduced conduction in GBS is taught directly.\n\nMuscle disease is wrong because the muscle disease lecture discusses primary muscle disorders with myopathic patterns and does not give them slowed nerve conduction; the nerve conduction delay belongs to the nerve, not the muscle fibre. Neuromuscular junction is wrong because MG is a post-synaptic neuromuscular transmission disorder in the lecture, whose electrophysiology is a decremental response on repetitive stimulation, not a marked nerve conduction delay. Anterior horn cell disease is wrong because the lecture places MND in the anterior horn cell family; motor neuron loss does not slow conduction along surviving myelinated fibres the way segmental demyelination does, and the claim distinguishing the two is not taken from the course material. The demyelinating category, with the lecture\'s reduced conduction velocity in GBS, makes demyelinating polyneuropathy the answer. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate marked nerve conduction delay with demyelinating polyneuropathy.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q262, book p.51; also printed as Q272 (book p.52), options reshuffled, key printed there as \'A\' — same content, folded, not drafted separately'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-263', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'A 33-year-old woman complains of diplopia in the early evening every day for the past month, which resolves following sleep. She also complains of jaw weakness after eating large meals. She has no significant past medical history. Physical examination is normal, except repeated blinking elicits a ptosis that resolves following the administration of IV edrophonium. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Botulism', 'Eaton-Lambert syndrome', 'Guillain-Barre syndrome', 'Multiple sclerosis', 'Myasthenia gravis'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'Myasthenia gravis is the keyed diagnosis. The muscle disease lecture defines MG as an autoimmune post-synaptic disorder of neuromuscular transmission manifested by fluctuating muscle fatigability and weakness that improves with rest, and it lists extra-ocular weakness with diplopia and ptosis, plus jaw weakness and difficult chewing due to bulbar affection, among the cardinal symptoms; it also states that fatigability is worse at the end of the day and with exercise, with transient improvement with rest, and can be demonstrated clinically by asking the patient to count to 100 or sustain upward gaze. The woman\'s diplopia in the early evening resolving after sleep, jaw weakness after large meals, and ptosis provoked by repeated blinking all reproduce this fatigability-worsens-at-day-end-and-with-exertion pattern, including the bulbar jaw involvement and a provocation manoeuvre analogous to the lecture\'s sustained-gaze test. The edrophonium test is the lecture\'s named diagnostic tool: edrophonium (Tensilon) is an acetylcholine esterase inhibitor given intravenously as 10 mg/1 ml, starting with a 2 mg test dose then 8 mg over one minute, and because MG weakness reflects a loss of functional post-synaptic acetylcholine receptors, transiently raising acetylcholine at the junction produces objective, if brief, improvement; here the ptosis resolves after IV edrophonium, exactly the response the lecture predicts. Every element of the vignette maps to MG.\n\nGuillain-Barre syndrome is wrong because the peripheral neuropathy lecture describes GBS as an acute onset of peripheral and cranial nerve dysfunction usually preceded by a viral respiratory or gastrointestinal infection 5 days to 4 weeks earlier, with rapidly progressive symmetric weakness and loss of tendon reflexes; this patient has no antecedent illness, a normal examination apart from ptosis, and a month-long diurnal fatigability pattern rather than an acute progressive areflexic weakness. Multiple sclerosis is listed only as a differential of MG in the muscle disease lecture, without its own features being taught there, so a positive edrophonium response with rest-relief of diplopia is not something the course material attributes to MS. Eaton-Lambert syndrome is also among the MG differential in the lecture, described as a pre-synaptic disorder due to antibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels, with an incremental EMG pattern on repetitive stimulation — the opposite of the electro-decremental response the lecture assigns to MG on repetitive nerve stimulation; Eaton-Lambert is remembered clinically for proximal limb weakness rather than the rest-responsive ocular and bulbar fatigability shown here, though that specific clinical contrast is not taken from the course material. Botulism is grouped in the lecture with poliomyelitis and diphtheria as a cause of acute bulbar weakness in the MG differential, and it does not show the day-end fatigability and edrophonium-responsive resolution this patient demonstrates; the specific botulism presentation and its lack of edrophonium response are not taken from the course material. The edrophonium response and diurnal fatigue together select MG over every listed distractor. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize MG from evening diplopia, bulbar fatigue, and an edrophonium response.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q263, book p.51'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-264', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'Which of the following features is characteristic of myasthenia gravis?',
  options:['Diplopia', 'Equal sex incidence', 'Fasciculation', 'Lid lag', 'Loss of pupillary reflexes'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Diplopia is the keyed characteristic feature. The muscle disease lecture lists intermittent and asymmetric extra-ocular muscle affection with diplopia and ptosis among the cardinal symptoms of myasthenia gravis, and it names ocular muscle weakness as the earliest and most frequent presentation. Diplopia caused by fatigable extra-ocular weakness is therefore a defining MG feature in the course material.\n\nEqual sex incidence is wrong because the lecture states that peaks of onset are seen in the 2nd and 3rd decades in females and in the 5th and 6th decades in males; the disease is not presented as having equal sex incidence. Fasciculation is wrong because the lecture describes fasciculation as a nicotinic adverse effect of edrophonium overdose and as a sign of cholinergic crisis from anticholinesterase excess in MG treatment; it is not a characteristic resting feature of the disease itself, and the lecture\'s Duchenne section notes no fasciculation in that myopathy, so fasciculation is not the taught MG hallmark. Lid lag is not a lecture feature of MG; the taught ocular findings are diplopia and ptosis, and lid lag\'s association is not taken from the course material. Loss of pupillary reflexes is wrong because the lecture does not list pupillary involvement among the MG cardinal symptoms, and the extra-ocular weakness it describes is fatigable diplopia and ptosis rather than loss of the pupillary reflex. The lecture\'s explicit pairing of diplopia with MG makes it the characteristic feature. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify diplopia as a characteristic ocular feature of myasthenia gravis.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q264, book p.51'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-265', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'In myasthenia gravis, the defectis:',
  options:['Presynaptic in calcium channels', 'Presynaptic acetylcholine vesicles', 'Post synaptic with acetylcholine receptors antibodies', 'Post synaptic affecting potassium channels', 'In the microtubules'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'Post synaptic with acetylcholine receptors antibodies is the keyed answer. The muscle disease lecture states that MG is an autoimmune disease mediated by T-lymphocytes with the development of autoantibodies to nicotinic acetylcholine receptors of the post-synaptic neuromuscular junction, or against muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK). It goes on to say these antibodies block the binding of acetylcholine molecules to their receptors or trigger immune-mediated degradation of the ACh receptors, with loss of large numbers of functional ACh receptors. The defect is therefore post-synaptic and antibody-driven, exactly matching the option.\n\nPresynaptic in calcium channels is wrong because the lecture assigns voltage-gated calcium channel antibodies to the pre-synaptic Lambert-Eaton syndrome, not to MG; it appears in the MG differential with an incremental EMG pattern. Presynaptic acetylcholine vesicles is likewise not the MG site in the lecture, which places the pathological process at the post-synaptic receptor; abnormal presynaptic vesicle release is not how the lecture explains MG. Post synaptic affecting potassium channels is wrong because the lecture\'s post-synaptic target is the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (and MuSK), not potassium channels, and no potassium-channel defect is described in MG there, so the claim is not taken from the course material. In the microtubules is not a neuromuscular junction structure in the lecture, and its relevance to MG or to any junction disorder is not taken from the course material. The autoimmune post-synaptic ACh-receptor antibody mechanism is the lecture\'s core MG teaching. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate myasthenia gravis with a post-synaptic ACh-receptor antibody defect.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q265, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-266', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'Anterior horn cell disease is:',
  options:['Myasthenia gravis', 'Progressive muscular atrophy', 'Tabes dorsalis', 'Botulism'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Progressive muscular atrophy is the keyed answer. The muscle disease lecture lists progressive spinal muscular atrophy (MND) as an anterior horn cell disorder of the motor neuron disease family, in the differential diagnosis of muscle dystrophy, and uses the anterior horn cell localization to distinguish motor neuron disease from primary muscle and junction disorders. Progressive muscular atrophy, the oldest term for a lower motor neuron degeneration, is therefore the option the bank places in the anterior horn cell category, and this nomenclature link is not taken from the course material but is the intended association.\n\nMyasthenia gravis is wrong because the lecture places MG at the post-synaptic neuromuscular junction with autoantibodies to acetylcholine receptors, not in the anterior horn cell body. Botulism is wrong because the muscle disease lecture groups botulism with poliomyelitis and diphtheria as causes of acute bulbar weakness in the MG differential, and botulinum toxin acts on presynaptic neurotransmitter release; the lecture does not call botulism anterior horn cell disease, so the mechanism distinction is not taken from the course material. Tabes dorsalis is not discussed in either lecture as an anterior horn cell disorder; it is historically a late syphilitic spinal cord syndrome, and its classification here is not taken from the course material. The lecture\'s separation of motor neuron disease, as an anterior horn cell process, from the junction and nerve disorders makes progressive muscular atrophy the only compatible option. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify progressive muscular atrophy as the anterior horn cell disease among the options.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q266, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-267', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'Tensilon test improves the muscle weakness in:',
  options:['Motor neuron disease', 'Myopathy', 'Myasthenia gravis', 'GBS'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'Myasthenia gravis is the keyed answer. The muscle disease lecture names the edrophonium (Tensilon) test among the diagnostic approach of myasthenia gravis: edrophonium is an acetylcholine esterase inhibitor given as 10 mg/1 ml IV, starting with 2 mg then 8 mg over one minute. Because MG weakness is caused by post-synaptic ACh-receptor loss, temporarily inhibiting acetylcholinesterase raises acetylcholine at the junction and transiently improves the weakness; the lecture\'s earlier statement that edrophonium would be expected to temporarily improve symptoms is the taught diagnostic response. The Tensilon test is therefore a diagnostic tool specifically for MG.\n\nMotor neuron disease is wrong because the lecture treats MND as an anterior horn cell disorder, and the edrophonium test is not part of its workup or of the MND discussion; an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor would not reverse anterior horn cell loss, though that mechanism point is not taken from the course material. Myopathy is wrong because primary muscle disorders such as inflammatory myopathies are not characterized by a junctional transmission deficit, and the lecture does not list Tensilon testing among myopathy diagnostic tools. GBS is wrong because the peripheral neuropathy lecture diagnoses GBS by history, electrophysiology, and CSF (elevated protein with normal cell count), not by edrophonium; Tensilon is not mentioned for GBS in either lecture. The junctional mechanism the lecture assigns to MG, with Tensilon as its named diagnostic and temporary-improvement test, selects myasthenia gravis. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate a positive Tensilon test with myasthenia gravis.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q267, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-268', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'Intermittent bulbar palsy is seen in:',
  options:['Snake bite', 'Myasthenia gravis', 'Poliomyelitis', 'Motor neurone disease'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Myasthenia gravis is the keyed answer. The muscle disease lecture lists bulbar affection in MG: soft speech with nasal tone, nasal regurgitation, and difficult chewing from bulbar involvement, with fatigability worse at the end of the day and transient improvement with rest. The "intermittent" character maps exactly to MG\'s defining fluctuating fatigability, which the lecture presents as its cardinal feature, so an intermittent bulbar palsy is the MG pattern rather than a fixed bulbar deficit. The course does not use the phrase intermittent bulbar palsy, but the fluctuating, rest-improving bulbar weakness it describes is the mechanism behind it; the direct equation of the two is the bank\'s intended association.\n\nMotor neurone disease is wrong because the lecture places MND in the anterior horn cell family as progressive muscular atrophy with a progressive rather than fluctuating course, and its differential position in the lecture is that of a chronic progressive disorder, so it would not be the cause of an intermittent palsy. Polliomyelitis is wrong because the lecture groups poliomyelitis only as a cause of acute bulbar weakness in the MG differential, and acute onset is not intermittent; the specific claim is not taken from the course material. Snake bite is also listed in the lecture only indirectly as an acute bulbar-differential context; it produces acute neuromuscular weakness of rapid onset rather than intermittent diurnal palsy, and that mechanistic distinction is not taken from the course material. The fluctuating bulbar weakness that improves with rest, which the lecture teaches for MG, makes MG the answer. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate intermittent bulbar weakness with myasthenia gravis\' fluctuating fatigability.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q268, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-269', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'Which of the following is associated with peripheral neuropathy?',
  options:['Hypotonia', 'Hyperreflexia', 'Hypertonia', 'Increased tendon reflexes'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Hypotonia is the keyed association. The peripheral neuropathy lecture describes polyneuropathy as a diffuse peripheral nerve lesion manifested by weakness, sensory loss, pain, and autonomic dysfunction, with weakness greatest in distal limb muscles and tendon reflexes often lost, especially in demyelinating neuropathy. A flaccid motor pattern with reduced or absent reflexes means reduced tone, i.e., hypotonia, because the lesion interrupts the final motor pathway of the lower motor neuron. The lecture\'s loss of tendon reflexes and distal weakness in neuropathy is the basis for selecting hypotonia, while the other options describe the opposite upper motor neuron phenotype.\n\nHyperreflexia, Hypertonia, and Increased tendon reflexes are all statements of the upper motor neuron pattern: increased tone and exaggerated tendon reflexes go with corticospinal tract disease, not with a peripheral neuropathy. The lecture never assigns these to neuropathy; it twice emphasizes loss of tendon reflexes in neuropathy (reflexes are often lost and the GBS section records lost tendon reflexes), and the Duchenne section reinforces that absent deep reflexes belong to lower motor unit disease. The claim that hyperreflexia and hypertonia characterize upper motor neuron rather than lower motor neuron disease is not taken from the course material but is the routine contrast the bank tests. Because peripheral neuropathy is a lower motor neuron phenomenon in the lecture, the associated finding is the reduced tone, hypotonia. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate peripheral neuropathy with the lower motor neuron sign of hypotonia.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q269, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-270', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'What is the most important complication in Myasthenia gravis?',
  options:['Cardiac arrhythmias', 'Respiratory embarrassment', 'Renal failure'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Respiratory embarrassment is the keyed complication. The muscle disease lecture lists respiratory muscle involvement with respiratory failure among the cardinal symptoms of myasthenia gravis, and it teaches that untreated MG, or MG precipitated by hot weather, inter-current illness, menstruation, pregnancy, thyrotoxicosis, or certain medications, leads to myasthenic crisis with respiratory failure. Respiratory embarrassment is therefore the lecture\'s stated most serious complication: the respiratory failure of a myasthenic crisis is the life-threatening endpoint, and the lecture\'s management section names plasma exchange as indicated in myasthenic crisis for precisely this situation. The peripheral neuropathy lecture similarly notes that in severe polyneuropathy, including GBS, the patient may become quadriplegic and respirator dependent, underscoring how respiratory weakness is the dangerous endpoint of neuromuscular disease.\n\nCardiac arrhythmias are not selected. The lecture associates arrhythmias with Duchenne muscular dystrophy and its cardiomyopathy, and with certain other dystrophies, but it does not make cardiac arrhythmia the major MG complication; the specific claim that MG is arrhythmia-prone is not taken from the course material. Renal failure is not discussed as a complication of MG in either lecture, so any role it might play is not taken from the course material. The decision tracks the lecture\'s own risk hierarchy: MG patients die of respiratory failure in crisis, making respiratory embarrassment the most important complication. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify respiratory failure as the most important complication of myasthenia gravis.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q270, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-271', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'That are characterized by multiplicity in place include all of the followings except:',
  options:['Behcet Disease', 'Myasthenia Gravis', 'CNS vascuitis e.g. SLE', 'Multiple sclerosis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Myasthenia Gravis is the keyed exception. The stem, with its printed defective wording, asks which option is NOT characterized by multiplicity in place, i.e., by disease at multiple separate sites. Multiple sclerosis is the lecture\'s multi-site disease: in the muscle disease lecture MS appears as a central nervous system disorder whose hallmark is lesions at multiple places and times, and the peripheral lecture table groups it nowhere because it is a CNS process. Behcet Disease is likewise a multi-site condition, with multisystem involvement across vessels and organs, and CNS vasculitis such as SLE is by definition a multi-focal vascular disease; both are treated here as multiplicity-in-place conditions, and the specific multi-site character of Behcet and of SLE-related CNS vasculitis is not taken from the course material.\n\nMyasthenia Gravis is the odd one because the muscle disease lecture locates MG at a single site of pathology, the post-synaptic neuromuscular junction, where autoantibodies to the acetylcholine receptor block transmission and cause fatigable weakness. Although MG affects multiple muscles functionally, its unifying anatomical defect is one junctional site, not multiplicity of disease in place. The lecture describes its defect as a single post-synaptic mechanism, which is the "place" concept the stem tests. The claim that MS, Behcet, and CNS vasculitis form the multiplicity group while MG does not is the bank\'s intended classification; the MS multi-site and MG single-site concepts are anchored in the lecture, and the Behcet and vasculitis specifics are tagged not taken from the course material. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize that myasthenia gravis lacks the multi-site lesion pattern of the other options.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q271, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-273', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-lmn',
  stem:'Which of the following also has a relapsing and remitting course?',
  options:['Myasthenia gravis.', 'Intracerebral hemorrhage.', 'Duchenne muscle dystrophy.', 'Guillian barre syndrome.'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Myasthenia gravis is the keyed answer because it has a relapsing and remitting course. The muscle disease lecture presents MG as fluctuating muscle fatigability and weakness that improve with rest, worse at the end of the day and with exercise, with transient improvement on rest; it also describes myasthenic crisis precipitated by intercurrent illness or stress, with improvement between episodes. This fluctuating, sometimes improving, sometimes worsening pattern is the relapsing-remitting course the stem refers to, and it is the lecture-supported reading of MG\'s waxing and waning symptoms. The specific phrase relapsing and remitting is not used in the lecture; the claim that MG is the relapsing and remitting disease among these four is the bank\'s intended classification.\n\nDuchenne muscle dystrophy is wrong because the lecture describes DMD as a chronic, genetically determined myopathy with a progressive course, worsening until the patient becomes wheelchair-dependent by mid-adolescence and respiratory failure in the third decade; there is no remission. Guillian barre syndrome, with its printed spelling, is wrong because the peripheral neuropathy lecture describes GBS as worsening for days to three weeks, then a period of stability, then gradual improvement, i.e., a monophasic illness, not repeated relapses and remissions. Intracerebral hemorrhage is wrong because it is an acute vascular event with sudden onset and fixed deficit; neither lecture discusses it, so its lack of a relapsing-remitting course is not taken from the course material. The differing courses — fluctuating MG versus progressive DMD versus monophasic GBS — select myasthenia gravis. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify myasthenia gravis as the relapsing-remitting illness among the options.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q273, book p.52'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-274', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-nerve',
  stem:'Bilateral lower motor neuron facial weakness can occur in:',
  options:['Duchene muscle dystrophy', 'Becker muscle dystrophy', 'Guillain Barre Syndrome', 'Diabetic polyneuropathy'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'Guillain Barre Syndrome is the keyed answer. The peripheral neuropathy lecture states that in GBS the cranial nerves may be affected, that more than 50% of patients have facial diplegia, dysphagia, and dysarthria, and that occasionally facial, ocular, or oropharyngeal muscles are affected first; it also notes that cranial nerves are particularly affected in GBS and diphtheritic neuropathy. The peripheral lecture\'s picture of acute bilateral cranial nerve weakness with symmetric limb arreflexia fits bilateral lower motor neuron facial weakness, and GBS is the option where the course explicitly teaches facial diplegia. The claim that bilateral facial weakness in GBS is a lower motor neuron, not an upper motor neuron, pattern is not taken from the course material but matches the arreflexic peripheral disease the lecture describes.\n\nDuchene and Becker muscle dystrophies, with their printed spellings, are wrong because the muscle disease lecture says the muscles of the face are not affected in Duchenne dystrophy, which is a primary myopathy sparing the face; Becker is described as the milder dystrophinopathy without facial weakness, and neither lecture gives Becker facial diplegia. Diabetic polyneuropathy is wrong because the lecture describes diabetic neuropathy as a distal symmetric sensory and sensorimotor process with painful burning sensations in the feet; facial weakness is not part of the taught diabetic picture, and its absence there is the relevant contrast. The lecture\'s explicit facial diplegia in GBS, with cranial nerve vulnerability in the disease, makes Guillain Barre Syndrome the cause of bilateral lower motor neuron facial weakness. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize GBS as a cause of bilateral lower motor neuron facial weakness.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 12 "Neuromuscular Disorders," Q274, book p.52'
}